 2. AI pixie dust is losing its stock market power 
 
 By Matt Phillips 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: FactSet; Chart: Matt Phillips/Axios Soaring sales, booming backlogs and gangbusters guidance from AI-related companies just doesn't cut the mustard in this market moment.

 Why it matters: The lackluster response to still strong AI-related earnings points to growing investor doubts about the durability of the boom.

 Catch up quick: A broad swath of AI-related shares took it on the chin last week, including:

 Hyperscaler Meta (down 9.4%). AI-construction plays like HVAC provider Comfort Systems (-19.3%) and engineering and construction giant Quanta (-13.5%). And AI power trades like turbine maker GE Vernova (-11.5%) and Bloom Energy (-12.7%). Between the lines: The slump came despite some of those very same companies reporting the kind of top-line Q3 results that likely would have received the market's version of a standing ovation last year. 

 Cases in point: Meta's third-quarter results Wednesday were great. It was a legit beat-and-raise . The stock plunged 7% the next day.

 A similar dynamic played out for two companies that produce equipment needed for data centers, with shares slipping after Comfort Systems and Western Digital reported strong results. The bottom line: If the AI trade stops working, it's quite hard to see how the overall market keeps any kind of forward momentum. Buckle up.

 
 
